WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.120
Hey Wisecrack, Jared IRL again.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.560
For those popping in for the first time, this
is still an experiment where we quickly respond

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.820
to developments in TV and Film as they happen-
not just Rick and Morty.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:16.650
So if you like them, or have a request, let
us know.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.660
The Vindicators 3: Return of the World Ender
was a pretty awesome jab at Marvel movies,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.690
with "The Vindicators” acting as an obvious
stand-in for "The Avengers", and Rick acting

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:30.410
as a mouthpiece for people disillusioned with
the rather formulaic state of popular cinema.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.340
"They’re the writers of their own press
releases, Morty.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.120
They’re a bunch of drama queens who spend
an hour talking and 20 minutes jumping around

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.120
while shit blows up.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.120
They’re a phase.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.120
We did one.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.320
It was the big event of that summer.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.020
Let it die.”

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.800
But instead of focusing on Rick’s beef with
our Disney overlords, we’re gonna set our

00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:46.950
sights a little higher today.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.760
Superhero films have been clumsily grasping
at the concepts of good and evil for what

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.280
seems like an eternity, and this episode of
Rick and Morty comments on this in some pretty

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.280
clever, and even profound ways.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:02.180
Welcome to this Wisecrack quicktake on Vindicators
3: The Return of the Worldender.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.780
And you guessed it- Spoilers ahead.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.139
First a recap: Morty gets to choose the next
adventure with Rick, so he decides to spend

00:01:09.139 --> 00:01:11.530
it with his heros: The Vindicators.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:16.829
In good Sanchezian fashion, Rick gets blackout
drunk, destroys the ominous worldender with

00:01:16.829 --> 00:01:21.439
little effort, and traps the Vindicators in
a series of trials that ruins them.

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:26.219
The real arc of The Vindicators 3, like another
“Morty adventure” we know - is Morty’s

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:28.829
optimistic worldview crumbling around him.

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:33.939
In the case of Meeseeks and Destroy, Morty’s
wholesome view of fairy tales erodes as the

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.400
genre is subverted.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.420
In this episode, Morty’s faith in the Vindicators
erodes as the superhero genre is subverted.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:51.900
But beneath all the world enders and superhero
jokes, this episode is also about the complexities

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:52.880
of good and evil.

00:01:52.889 --> 00:01:56.459
Unlike some adventures where Morty’s view
of the world is shattered, Rick plays

00:01:56.459 --> 00:01:59.789
a unique role in making his worldview disintegrate
here.

00:01:59.789 --> 00:02:05.079
After killing Worldender while blackout drunk,
Rick creates a Saw-inspired puzzle that is

00:02:05.079 --> 00:02:10.160
less “a thought out plan to make people
confront their demons” a la Jigsaw and more

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.400
“a drunken scheme to push people over a
moral cliff.”

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.780
And it works: Vance show his true colors,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.380
Million Ants kill Allen Rails

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.440
over a woman, and Crocubot brings out the
Vindicator’s little secret.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.060
By the end, Rick gets to lord over Morty about
the sham that is heroism.

00:02:41.069 --> 00:02:44.459
“I appreciate it morty I know you were sucking
the Kool aid out of the vindicators' d**ks

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.510
so the fact that I was right about them was
pretty hard to admit."

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:48.500
"Yeah it is."

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:52.140
What’s really clever about this episode
is how they draw from some of the more unique

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.200
villains in popular cinema.

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:05.159
Unlike most antagonists, Jigsaw isn’t about
senseless murder or financial gain, he wants

00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:10.099
to change people’s paradigms by making them
endure suffering - in some cases, to make

00:03:10.099 --> 00:03:11.700
them appreciate life more.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.879
Similarly, Drunk Rick’s trials aren’t
meant primarily to eradicate The Vindicators,

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:21.360
but rather to create a moral crisis for them,
proving to Morty that they are NOT heros.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.100
Now, I thought this was pretty cool in itself,
but what I thought really elevated this episode

00:03:31.109 --> 00:03:34.370
was a less obvious inspiration: The Joker.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:39.239
Morty’s trip in the carnival ride seems
inspired by Alan Moore’s The Killing Joke,

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:41.650
when Commissioner Gordon is tortured by the
Joker.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.549
Like Jigsaw, The Joker isn’t only motivated
by murder.

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:50.660
He’s out to prove that with one sufficiently
bad day, Gordon could become just as mad as him.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.420
As another Joker inspired by the Killing Joke
puts it.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:58.180
"See madness, as you know, is like gravity."

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:02.440
"All it takes is a little push."

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.960
And what is Rick’s shenanigans in this episode
but to push the Vindicators to lose their s***?

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:13.760
Just as The Joker infects the once idealistic
Harvey with his nihilistic philosophy by killing

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.140
Rachel and turning corrupt cops against him,
so too does Rick turn the benevolent Vindicators

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:25.080
into violent backstabbers- thus upending Morty’s
simplistic understanding of good and evil.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.160
"Oh, I don't know, you managed to destroy just about everything today,

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.160
the villains, the heroes, the lines between them...

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:30.820
my childhood."

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:31.980
"Thank youuuuu"

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.760
Ok, so Rick doesn’t believe really buy that
“heroes are good.”

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:39.610
But there’s a larger point about how the
way we’re spoon-fed narratives of good and

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.289
evil is actually...super bad.

00:04:42.289 --> 00:04:45.900
You know, narratives like this one “Everyone
in the universe is a hero.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:49.220
All you have to do is notice the difference
between good and bad and root for good!”

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.569
But thinking in such simple terms can lead
to problems.

00:04:52.569 --> 00:04:57.139
For Philosopher Jacques Ranciere, the problem
with “Evil” with a big capital E - you

00:04:57.139 --> 00:05:01.289
know, the kind in every single superhero movie is that

00:05:01.289 --> 00:05:06.000
any kind of conflict becomes the ultimate
struggle of Good and Evil, the value

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.660
of winning that battle becomes, well, infinite.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:13.820
It’s a fight for the soul of the world,
or galaxy, or universe - all or nothing.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:17.740
So people can do even the WORST sh** in the
world to achieve victory.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.600
But, as the logic goes, isn’t it justified?

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:25.280
What’s so bad about letting a few thousands
people die if it means destroying ultimate evil?

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:26.520
Well...

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.520
"What happened on Dorian 5?"

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:29.460
"Nothing!"

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:32.300
"Nothing?! We exterminated a PLANET!"

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.500
"W-wait, huh?"

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:39.700
"Doomnomatron was hiding there, he's a shapeshifter! Destroying Dorian 5 was the only way to kill him!"

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:43.840
"I could have made you a device to detect Doomnomatron from orbit like that."

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:45.980
What does this say about the Vindicators?

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:51.260
To summarize Ranciere: the problem with “infinite”
evil - whether in the form of world-destroyers

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:55.550
or mortal humans- is they warrant “infinite
justice” - the kind that doesn’t really

00:05:55.550 --> 00:06:01.300
care about morals, or the people of Dorian
5, and often even loses sight of the “justice” part.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:06.500
By the end of the episode Supernova has also
been infected with Rick’s nihilistic philosophy,

00:06:06.509 --> 00:06:09.629
no longer believing in a real division between
good and evil.

00:06:09.629 --> 00:06:14.680
However, she recognizes the simplistic division
must be upheld

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:15.680
"It's like you said Morty."

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:16.980
"There’s no right or wrong."

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:17.680
"Never said that."

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:22.600
"It’s the galaxy’s faith in the vindicators
that keeps the galaxy secure."

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:27.460
She’s willing to kill Rick and Morty, and
even her LOVER to protect the perception that

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:28.979
the Vindicator’s are good.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:33.780
It’s not their actions that matter, it’s
the galaxy’s faith in their actions that does.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:38.040
I’m pretty sure this another shoutout to The
Dark Knight, which is probably the best example

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:40.539
of moral greyness in a comic book movie.

00:06:40.539 --> 00:06:44.560
Where Batman lies about the “goodness”
of Harvey Dent to maintain peace and order

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:48.660
in Gotham, Supernova wants the galaxy to believe in
a similar lie about herself.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:53.550
In both cases, people are advocating for a
“noble lie,” the idea that societies can

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:59.200
be built on a myth that maintains order - an
idea made famous by the Greek philosopher Plato.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:01.840
And it’s not the first time Rick and Morty
has made this kind of

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:03.840
Dark Knight-esque/noble lie shoutout.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:09.260
"We found it inside a lockbox. Inside King Jellybean's closet."

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.260
"We have to tell the people!"

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.500
"Wait, destroy it."

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:25.740
"Our people will get more from the idea he represented, than from the Jellybean he actually was."

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.260
Now, this is kind of philosophy 101, but it’s
worth noting at least because of its relation

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:31.260
to The Dark Knight.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:35.939
But where The Dark Knight kind of celebrates
this lie, Rick and Morty uses it to deconstruct

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:37.580
the myth of superheroes.

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.840
"Rick says good and bad are artificial constructs."

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.200
"Yeah well I get the feeling... he kinda needs that to be the case."

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:48.860
However, rather than JUST deconstructing good
and evil as arbitrary concepts, which, by

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:50.160
the way, it’s done before,

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.060
the show is also
suggesting that the complete absence of these

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:59.040
concepts isn’t so great either.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:04.080
This is a clever call back to the last episode,
where Dr. Wong confronts Rick in saying

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:08.760
"The only connection between your unquestionable intelligence and the sickness destroying your family"

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:13.580
"is that everyone in your family, you included, use intelligence to justify sickness."

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:18.220
Whereas the vindicators fail to critically
engage with their methods, or even their mission,

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:23.720
Rick refuses to engage with his decisions
for the exact opposite reason: because nothing matters.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:28.020
And if nothing matters, and good and evil
are just social constructs, then he can’t

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:29.560
consider his actions evil.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:32.270
He can just continue doing whatever the f***
he wants.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:37.080
So the episode presents us with two differing
perspectives regarding good and evil, both

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:41.860
of which are, in typical Rick and Morty fashion,
completely dismal.

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:46.150
We either have zero conviction of the division
between good and evil, which leads to Rick

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:51.440
doing whatever they f*** he wants and it not
mattering, or we have complete and utter conviction

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:56.640
of what constitutes good and evil, and people end up
doing really messed up shit for the sake of good…

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.640
like blow up an entire planet.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.360
As always, in Rick and Morty,

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.530
There are no right answers.

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:03.530
Only wrong ones.

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:08.940
If you want to hear more depressing Rick and
Morty philosophy, check out our Rick and Morty podcast “The Squanch".

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:13.380
We’re breaking down every episode in detail,
answering your questions, and you’ll even

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.530
get to hear the joker impression I’ve been
working on for nearly a decade.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:19.220
It’s getting there.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:22.680
Click on the link below, or subscribe wherever
your get podcasts.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:23.880
Stay good, Wisecrack. Peace!

